    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,-4,0)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{3x}y - 3x^2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(3,-4,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,-4).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,-4) & =\\frac{3(3)}{(-4)}-3(3)^2(-4) \\\\  & =(- \\frac{9}{4})+(108) \\\\  & =\\frac{423}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/id": "9bb0f096-d343-50e6-ab19-61c16c1aaf3c",
